<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rabbi Akiva</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 50–135</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century">2nd century</a> · Also known as Akiva ben Joseph, Rabbi Akiba</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rabbi Akiva (Akiva ben Joseph) was one of the leading Jewish sages of the early 2nd century CE, whose systematic teaching of oral law laid foundations for the Mishnah. Rabbinic tradition says he hailed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-bar-kokhba">Simon bar Kokhba</a> as the Messiah. He was executed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> around 135 CE for continuing to teach the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torah">Torah</a>, and is remembered as a martyr.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rabbi-akiva.jpg" alt="Rabbi Akiva" width="320" height="478" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Akiva.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Akiva ben Joseph was born about 50 CE to humble parents, and according to some sources he descended from converts to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talmud">Talmud</a> tells that he was an unlettered shepherd in the service of Ben Kalba Sabuaʿ, a wealthy citizen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, when the man's daughter, named Rachel in a later version, offered to marry him if he would begin to study <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torah">Torah</a>.</p><p>Her father drove her from his house, and the couple lived in great poverty. Another tradition has Akiva, at the age of 40, attend the academy of his native town, Lod, headed by Eliezer ben Hurcanus. He also studied under Joshua ben Hananiah and Nachum Ish Gamzu, and later moved his own school to Beneberak.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Akiva was a member of the Sanhedrin that determined the final canon of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a>, and he played a decisive part in the inclusion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-of-songs">Song of Songs</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecclesiastes">Ecclesiastes</a>. He is called Rosh la-Hakhamim, Chief of the Sages. He worked on the Halakha, systematising its traditional material and developing a method of exegesis. He held that in the language of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torah">Torah</a> nothing is mere form, so that every peculiarity of diction, every particle and every letter carries meaning. Tradition holds that he arranged the Mishnah, and the Mishnah of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a> ha-Nasi and most of the halakhic Midrashim are regarded as derived from his school.</p><p>The leading tannaim of the middle of the second century came from his school, among them Rabbi Meir, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a> bar Ilai and Simeon bar Yochai. The Aggadah gives the number of his disciples variously as 12,000, 24,000 and 48,000. He supported Rabban Gamaliel II's aim of a central authority but held that the patriarch's power was limited by the written and oral law. He was in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 95-96 CE, and before 110 in Nehardea. His disciple Aquila gave Greek-speaking Jews a rabbinical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Accounts of Akiva's death vary, but all agree that he defied a Roman prohibition on teaching <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torah">Torah</a>, that the consul Turnus Rufus, as Jewish sources call him, ordered his execution, that he was flayed alive and that his last words were the Shema.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talmud">Babylonian Talmud</a> he explains to his students that he had long wondered how he could fulfil the command to love God 'with all your soul', and he extended the final word Echad, meaning One, until his life expired. He was executed in the aftermath of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-bar-kokhba">Bar Kokhba</a> revolt, on 28 September 135 CE. Tradition also holds that he alone of four sages who entered the Pardes absorbed its wisdom.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rabbi Akiva” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Rabbi Akiva</b>, in the 1st and 2nd centuries, forbade its use in popular celebrations, saying that whoever sang it in wine taverns as a vulgar song lost his share in the world to come, yet he defended it as the &quot;Holy of Holies&quot; of the Writings.</blockquote><a href="/songs/song-of-songs">Song of Songs</a></li><li><blockquote>Its leader, Simon bar Kosiba, was hailed as Bar Kokhba, 'son of the star', a messianic title reportedly endorsed by <b>Rabbi Akiva</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/bar-kokhba-state">Bar Kokhba state</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Lod, in what is now <a href="/countries/israel">Israel</a>. Died in Caesarea Maritima, in what is now <a href="/countries/israel">Israel</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=31.97,34.89&amp;year=105" aria-label="Lod on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5448 1001 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Rabbi Akiva"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5898 1271h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5898 1271h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=31.97,34.89&amp;year=105">What was here in 105 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Everything is foreseen, yet free will is given…&#8221;</p><footer>Sayings of the Fathers (Pirke Avot) 3:19 &#183; tr. Joseph I. Gorfinkle, 1913 &#183; as the Mishnah records his saying &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/8547/pg8547-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Beloved is man, for he was created in the image (of God)…&#8221;</p><footer>Sayings of the Fathers (Pirke Avot) 3:18 &#183; tr. Joseph I. Gorfinkle, 1913 &#183; as the Mishnah records his saying &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/8547/pg8547-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 540 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
